22.4  tmux: sessions that survive a lost connection

TL;DR
  - Closing an SSH connection hangs up its shell, and the hang-up signal stops
    what you started there.
  - tmux runs your shells in a server that keeps going: detach, log out, come
    back later, attach, and everything is where you left it.
  - It also splits one terminal into windows and panes.

      tmux new -s work          # start a session named work
                                # Ctrl-b d: detach
      tmux ls                   # sessions still running
      tmux attach -t work

Pitfalls
  - Every tmux shortcut starts with the prefix, Ctrl-b by default: Ctrl-b then c
    for a new window, % or " to split, [ to scroll.
  - For a single command, nohup cmd > out.log 2>&1 & is enough: it ignores the
    hang-up signal (10.2).
  - Some systems kill all your processes at logout (KillUserProcesses=yes in
    logind): tmux dies too, unless loginctl enable-linger.
  - On a connection that drops all the time, mosh reconnects by itself and can
    carry tmux.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why does logging out kill my processes?

    When the connection closes, the terminal goes away and the kernel sends
    SIGHUP to the session's processes; most stop on it. tmux's server isn't
    attached to your SSH terminal: it owns its own pseudo-terminals and only
    displays them in yours, so losing yours changes nothing for it.

  ▾ tmux or screen?

    GNU screen is older and found everywhere; tmux has a cleaner config and
    scripting (tmux send-keys, tmux split-window), and is the more common choice
    today. Both detach and reattach the same way.

Related
  Commands  tmux, nohup
  Paths     ~/.tmux.conf
  See also  idk 10.2 Signals
  See also  idk 22.1 SSH keys and the agent
